  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nThe equivalence between `<img>` and `<image>` opening tags is defined on the parsing level of the HTML syntax, so a static HTML document can never have an element with the local name `image` in the HTML namespace.\n\nNevertheless, this assumption does not hold if such an element occurs within an XML document, or is inserted by a script. User agents disagree on the DOM interface of this element, with Chrome having an `HTMLUnknownElement`, while Firefox gives `HTMLElement`.\n\n**Proposal**: For least surprise, it should follow that `<img>` and `<image>` are functionally equivalent in all circumstances, with the DOM interface of the latter having the same functionality — possibly via a new interface inheriting from `HTMLImageElement`.",
